<SiteLayout title="What is a tide? · 3Dweather" path="/what-is-a-tide" nav="guides"
  description="Why the sea rises and falls, spring and neap tides, and how tide times are predicted.">
  <article class="article">
    <h1>What is a tide?</h1>
    <p class="lede">The slow rise and fall of the sea, driven by the Moon and the Sun.</p>
    <h2>The pull of the Moon and the Sun</h2>
    <p>The Moon's gravity pulls a little harder on the side of the Earth facing it than on the far side. The
      difference stretches the oceans into two bulges, one under the Moon and one opposite it. As the Earth turns,
      a coast passes through both bulges, so most places see two high waters and two low waters a day. Because the
      Moon moves along its orbit while the Earth turns, the cycle takes about 24 hours 50 minutes, so the tides
      come about 50 minutes later each day.</p>
    <p>The Sun does the same at a little under half the strength.</p>
    <h2>Spring and neap tides</h2>
    <p>Around new and full moon the Sun and Moon pull in line and their bulges add up: high waters are higher
      and low waters lower. These are <em>spring tides</em> (nothing to do with the season). Around the quarter
      moons they pull at right angles and partly cancel: the range is smallest, the <em>neap tides</em>. The
      <a href="/tidal-coefficient">tidal coefficient</a> puts a number on where in this cycle a day falls.</p>
    <h2>Why every coast is different</h2>
    <p>The real ocean is not a smooth ball of water. Continents block the bulges, basins resonate, and the tide
      travels round each ocean as a wave. Some coasts get one tide a day, some two equal tides, many two unequal
      ones. Bays and estuaries can amplify the range to over ten metres, while some seas barely move.</p>
    <h2>How tides are predicted</h2>
    <p>Each place's tide can be written as a sum of regular waves, the <em>harmonic constituents</em>, each tied to
      a motion of the Moon or Sun: M2 (the principal lunar, every 12 h 25 min), S2 (solar, every 12 h), K1 and O1
      (daily) and dozens more. Measure the tide at a place for long enough and you know the size and timing of
      each wave there; add them up for any future date and you have a prediction.</p>
    <p>On this site, NOAA tide gauges use NOAA's published constituents; places near a NOAA gauge use NOAA's
      time and height corrections or that gauge's tide; everywhere else uses the EOT20 global ocean tide model.
      Each station page says which. Heights are above mean sea level.</p>
    <h2>What predictions leave out</h2>
    <p>Predictions are the astronomical tide only. Wind, air pressure and river flow can raise or lower the sea
      by tens of centimetres, and storm surges by metres. Our tides are not NOAA's official predictions and are
      not for navigation.</p>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
